.Dd September 8, 2026
.Dt DOASEDIT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm doasedit
.Nd edit files with root privileges using an unprivileged editor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm doasedit
.Op Fl h
.Op Fl -
.Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
allows editing files that are not editable by the current user.
.Pp
For each file,
.Nm
creates a private, unreadable temporary directory and copies the file
into it, then starts the editor on the copy in the user context.
Once the editor exits, if the temporary file has been modified, the
changes are written back to the original file with the least amount
of privilege possible.
Doas is called only when a privileged operation is actually needed,
and always with a fixed command invocation
.Pq never through a shell .
.Pp
The write-back replaces the file atomically and preserves its owner,
group and permission bits.
If the file is replaced or otherwise altered by another process while
it is being edited, the changes are refused rather than written over
the unexpected file.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl Fl help
Prints usage information, then exits.
.It Fl -
Indicates that no more options will be provided.
Everything after the delimiter is treated as a file.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width DOAS_EDITOR
.It Ev DOAS_EDITOR
The program used to edit files.
.It Ev VISUAL
The program used to edit files if
.Ev DOAS_EDITOR
is unset.
.It Ev EDITOR
The program used to edit files if
.Ev DOAS_EDITOR
and
.Ev VISUAL
are unset.
.It Ev TMPDIR
Directory for the private temporary files; defaults to
.Pa /tmp .
.El
.Pp
The editor command may contain arguments, separated by whitespace.
It is executed directly with
.Xr execvp 3 ;
no shell is involved, so shell quoting, globbing and redirections are
not interpreted.
If the editor starts an existing instance and returns immediately
.Pq typical of some graphical editors ,
.Nm
will compare the file before the edit finished and report it as
unchanged.
Append a command line argument that forces a new instance in that
case.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/doas.conf
.It Pa /etc/doas.conf
.Xr doas 1
configuration file.
After editing it
.Pq or a file below Pa /etc/doas.d/ ,
.Nm
parses the edited copy with
.Nm doas Fl C
and, on a syntax error, offers to edit again, overwrite anyway, or
abort, so that a mistake cannot lock the user out.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 if at least one file was edited successfully, and 1
otherwise, including when:
.Bl -bullet
.It
no arguments are passed,
.It
invalid arguments are passed,
.It
the program is run as root,
.It
no usable editor is found,
.It
a file cannot be read, validated or written back,
.It
three failed password attempts were made when writing a file.
.El
.Sh DOAS PERMISSIONS
The following doas invocations are used:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Nm doas Cm dd Ar status=none count=0 of=/dev/null
.Pq initial privilege check
.It
.Nm doas Cm cat Ar file
.Pq only when the file is not readable by the user
.It
.Nm doas Cm install Fl o Ar uid Fl g Ar gid Fl m Ar mode Ar tmpfile Ar file
.Pq write-back of modified files
.It
.Nm doas Fl C Ar tmpfile
.Pq syntax check of an edited doas configuration
.El
.Pp
Using
.Cm persist
in
.Xr doas.conf 5
avoids repeated password prompts.
.Sh SECURITY
.Nm
never runs an editor, or any other program, with elevated
privileges; privilege is confined to the fixed doas invocations
listed above.
Temporary files are created with
.Xr mkdtemp 3
in a private directory and are not shared with other users; files are
opened with
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW
and their identity
.Pq device, inode, mode, owner, group
is verified before and after every privileged step.
Files in user-writable locations cannot be used to gain write access
to arbitrary files because the write-back is performed with an atomic
rename in the destination directory, which replaces a symbolic link
rather than following it.
See
.Sh LOCALE
The interface of
.Nm
is U.S. English only, and its output is always UTF-8.
The only supported locale is
.Ev en_US.UTF-8 .
Setting
.Ev LANG ,
.Ev LANGUAGE ,
.Ev LC_ALL ,
.Ev LC_MESSAGES
or any other locale variable has no effect on the interface language,
prompts or diagnostics.
Legacy encodings such as ISO-8859-1 and Shift-JIS are not supported.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr doas 1 ,
.Xr doas.conf 5
